WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.983 --> 00:00:07.316
(static and music going in and out)

00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:18.030
- I was scrolling back in time recently

00:00:18.030 --> 00:00:21.570
past the billions of
little moments I've lived,

00:00:21.570 --> 00:00:24.960
all these photos that I've
captured over the years,

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:28.220
and I stumbled upon a
region of my photo bank

00:00:28.220 --> 00:00:31.320
that I hadn't really
ever looked at before.

00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:33.000
It was right here.

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:34.200
We had just gotten married

00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:36.100
and we walked from our little apartment

00:00:36.100 --> 00:00:38.070
up to the mountain near our house.

00:00:38.070 --> 00:00:39.358
How are you doing, Is?

00:00:39.358 --> 00:00:40.191
- Good.

00:00:40.191 --> 00:00:41.590
- We had no idea if we could
actually get to the top

00:00:41.590 --> 00:00:44.093
or how long it would take, but we hiked.

00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:47.890
We camped at the top
and we watched the sun

00:00:47.890 --> 00:00:49.403
fall over the mountain,

00:00:50.380 --> 00:00:54.083
and I just love looking at these photos.

00:00:55.410 --> 00:00:59.400
A few weeks later, Is
surprised me with a road trip

00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:01.919
to San Diego and then into Tijuana.

00:01:01.919 --> 00:01:04.600
- Crossing the border to Tijuana.

00:01:04.600 --> 00:01:07.400
- I had completely forgotten
about this experience.

00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:10.690
We spent so many hours on
the beach playing Frisbee,

00:01:10.690 --> 00:01:13.410
we ate tacos, we visited old friends.

00:01:13.410 --> 00:01:17.530
There was this political
rally that we wandered into.

00:01:17.530 --> 00:01:21.960
Man, I just, I don't even remember that,

00:01:21.960 --> 00:01:23.293
and then suddenly I do.

00:01:28.490 --> 00:01:33.317
That was, quote, "One of the
happiest days of my life."

00:01:35.320 --> 00:01:36.153
Okay.

00:01:41.330 --> 00:01:45.590
When I look at these
photos, at these videos,

00:01:45.590 --> 00:01:47.643
something happens in my brain.

00:01:49.850 --> 00:01:54.850
It's like I'm mourning something
and yet celebrating it too.

00:01:55.270 --> 00:01:57.900
I've been thinking about
this feeling for a while

00:01:57.900 --> 00:01:59.870
and I've been talking to
my friend Nathaniel Drew,

00:01:59.870 --> 00:02:03.830
who also is very interested
in investigating this.

00:02:03.830 --> 00:02:06.930
- So hold on, do you want to,

00:02:06.930 --> 00:02:08.220
I feel like I'm overthinking this

00:02:08.220 --> 00:02:11.070
but I want you to be happy
with the framing that I've got,

00:02:11.070 --> 00:02:11.903
at least frame rate.

00:02:11.903 --> 00:02:13.240
I'm shooting in 24.

00:02:13.240 --> 00:02:15.121
- As video nerds, dude,
we can't help ourselves

00:02:15.121 --> 00:02:18.850
to talk about composition and
frame rate right at the top.

00:02:18.850 --> 00:02:21.100
But yeah, no, I think
we're on the same page.

00:02:23.690 --> 00:02:26.530
- My theory on nostalgia

00:02:26.530 --> 00:02:30.230
is that nostalgia is
kind of like fire, okay?

00:02:30.230 --> 00:02:31.630
And it can be kind of
like playing with fire.

00:02:31.630 --> 00:02:33.890
So fire can, it can warm you up,

00:02:33.890 --> 00:02:38.050
but you, I feel like you
can get lost in it too,

00:02:38.050 --> 00:02:40.330
staring too long at the
fire or getting too close,

00:02:40.330 --> 00:02:41.280
you can get burned.

00:02:42.460 --> 00:02:45.310
- As we talked, we sort
of laid out our theories

00:02:45.310 --> 00:02:48.860
of what's going on here,
and then we decided

00:02:48.860 --> 00:02:50.920
how we could investigate this further

00:02:50.920 --> 00:02:53.150
by experimenting on ourselves.

00:02:53.150 --> 00:02:54.540
What would happen to my brain

00:02:54.540 --> 00:02:58.860
if I started listening to music from 2005

00:03:00.450 --> 00:03:05.450
and watching old home videos
and smelling old smells

00:03:06.420 --> 00:03:10.653
and just sparking that feeling every day?

00:03:13.220 --> 00:03:15.770
- I may just make a guess here.

00:03:15.770 --> 00:03:18.370
I do wonder if this will lead to you

00:03:18.370 --> 00:03:20.600
kind of returning to your
roots, whatever that means.

00:03:20.600 --> 00:03:23.950
- So Nathaniel and I set
out on separate journeys,

00:03:23.950 --> 00:03:27.540
both of us trying to spark
a certain type of nostalgia

00:03:27.540 --> 00:03:28.550
in our own way.

00:03:28.550 --> 00:03:30.920
Nathaniel's goal was to go back in time

00:03:30.920 --> 00:03:33.830
and start to experience the
world in a more analog way

00:03:33.830 --> 00:03:37.260
without social media and
phones and et cetera.

00:03:37.260 --> 00:03:40.420
And for me, the journey
was going to be getting out

00:03:40.420 --> 00:03:45.420
old journals, old books,
old smells, old music,

00:03:45.430 --> 00:03:47.760
and cultivating a
practice where every day,

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:51.410
I expose myself to this old stuff

00:03:51.410 --> 00:03:53.350
and see what that does to my brain.

00:03:53.350 --> 00:03:54.850
I dived into the neuroscience

00:03:54.850 --> 00:03:56.450
and looked at the studies
that have been done

00:03:56.450 --> 00:03:58.420
on what happens to the brain.

00:03:58.420 --> 00:04:00.670
But I also looked at my
own personal experience

00:04:00.670 --> 00:04:03.010
and I learned a lot, and
that's what I want to share

00:04:03.010 --> 00:04:03.843
with you today.

00:04:08.790 --> 00:04:13.370
So I have a theory on how
all of us experience life.

00:04:13.370 --> 00:04:14.793
It looks like this.

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:20.470
Okay, so you were born here.

00:04:20.470 --> 00:04:22.270
My theory is that as you grow up,

00:04:22.270 --> 00:04:25.270
you start experiencing
the world like this.

00:04:25.270 --> 00:04:27.360
This little ring represents the stuff

00:04:27.360 --> 00:04:30.940
that you're thinking about,
worrying about, excited about.

00:04:30.940 --> 00:04:33.890
This ring stretches maybe a few days back

00:04:33.890 --> 00:04:37.020
and forward in time, maybe a week or two,

00:04:37.020 --> 00:04:38.420
but not much more than that.

00:04:40.080 --> 00:04:42.810
If you were living totally
in the present moment,

00:04:42.810 --> 00:04:45.200
it would look something like this.

00:04:45.200 --> 00:04:47.930
If you were constantly
thinking about everything,

00:04:47.930 --> 00:04:49.700
everything that has
happened over the course

00:04:49.700 --> 00:04:52.860
of your entire year or your life,

00:04:52.860 --> 00:04:55.053
the ring would look a lot more like this.

00:04:58.870 --> 00:05:01.560
But I believe most people's
conscious experience

00:05:01.560 --> 00:05:03.820
looks like this, thinking about the good

00:05:03.820 --> 00:05:06.410
and stressful things that
happened yesterday at work

00:05:06.410 --> 00:05:08.890
or worrying about the
good and stressful things

00:05:08.890 --> 00:05:11.970
that are coming up tomorrow
or maybe next week.

00:05:11.970 --> 00:05:13.940
We have a productive day at work,

00:05:13.940 --> 00:05:15.770
and then you have a
really good conversation

00:05:15.770 --> 00:05:18.670
with your sister on the phone one evening,

00:05:18.670 --> 00:05:21.253
and then you also get in
a fight with your partner,

00:05:23.570 --> 00:05:25.670
and I believe that most
of us use the information

00:05:25.670 --> 00:05:30.400
in this ring to construct the
current story of ourselves.

00:05:30.400 --> 00:05:32.240
In other words, we use
this information to decide

00:05:32.240 --> 00:05:36.033
what we think and feel about
ourselves, who we think we are.

00:05:37.700 --> 00:05:40.330
But as we move forward,
these events will fade

00:05:40.330 --> 00:05:42.900
and new events will come into the ring

00:05:42.900 --> 00:05:44.970
and help us construct a new story

00:05:44.970 --> 00:05:48.653
of who we think we are, what
we feel about ourselves.

00:05:54.900 --> 00:05:57.700
So you're moving along,
and then one night,

00:05:57.700 --> 00:05:59.160
something really good happens.

00:05:59.160 --> 00:06:03.460
You go out with friends, you
dance, you have so much fun.

00:06:03.460 --> 00:06:04.980
It's the first week of October

00:06:04.980 --> 00:06:07.870
and the air is turning cool and crisp.

00:06:07.870 --> 00:06:10.630
It smells like fall, it
smells like the city,

00:06:10.630 --> 00:06:13.730
it smells like liquor,
like perfume, like freedom.

00:06:13.730 --> 00:06:16.450
You have this one conversation
that is so exciting,

00:06:16.450 --> 00:06:18.200
so inspiring.

00:06:18.200 --> 00:06:20.763
You feel connected to this
place, to these people.

00:06:21.600 --> 00:06:24.150
And then it's over, and for a few days,

00:06:24.150 --> 00:06:26.300
you consciously remember that night,

00:06:26.300 --> 00:06:28.060
you fondly think about it.

00:06:28.060 --> 00:06:30.520
But a week later, you
kind of have no reason

00:06:30.520 --> 00:06:32.220
to think about it anymore.

00:06:32.220 --> 00:06:33.950
That night sort of starts to fade,

00:06:33.950 --> 00:06:36.970
those conversations, that
feeling, those smells.

00:06:36.970 --> 00:06:38.810
Those memories are still stored away

00:06:38.810 --> 00:06:41.020
but your conscious mind has new stuff

00:06:41.020 --> 00:06:42.310
to put its spotlight on.

00:06:42.310 --> 00:06:44.700
That night, as a memory, as an experience,

00:06:44.700 --> 00:06:47.750
gets filed away in some
dusty file cabinet somewhere

00:06:47.750 --> 00:06:49.750
in the depths of your long-term memory.

00:06:49.750 --> 00:06:52.310
And then suddenly, it's
a time in your life

00:06:52.310 --> 00:06:55.460
that looks so different
from that great night

00:06:55.460 --> 00:06:57.470
you had with friends.

00:06:57.470 --> 00:07:01.840
You haven't seen friends in
a long time, you're lonely.

00:07:01.840 --> 00:07:04.950
You're wondering if you've
even ever had friends.

00:07:04.950 --> 00:07:07.430
You're wondering if you're even likable.

00:07:07.430 --> 00:07:10.980
You're going off the information
that's available to you,

00:07:10.980 --> 00:07:12.400
the stuff that's in this ring,

00:07:12.400 --> 00:07:17.163
which, on both sides, is full
of dull, quiet loneliness.

00:07:19.100 --> 00:07:21.550
But what if somehow you
could retrieve that night

00:07:21.550 --> 00:07:25.050
from the old dusty file cabinet
in your long-term memory?

00:07:25.050 --> 00:07:26.370
You dust it off.

00:07:26.370 --> 00:07:28.020
You maybe edit it a little bit

00:07:28.020 --> 00:07:30.960
so that it just includes the best parts,

00:07:30.960 --> 00:07:33.730
and then you bring it forth
and put it in the ring.

00:07:33.730 --> 00:07:37.223
Something happens to your
brain when you do this.

00:07:40.640 --> 00:07:42.960
Suddenly you have more information to add

00:07:42.960 --> 00:07:45.670
to your story about yourself.

00:07:45.670 --> 00:07:48.980
This information is old
information, but it feels new

00:07:48.980 --> 00:07:51.680
because you haven't thought
about it in a really long time

00:07:51.680 --> 00:07:54.430
and it challenges your current story,

00:07:54.430 --> 00:07:57.670
the story about you being
lonely and unlikable.

00:07:57.670 --> 00:08:01.460
And in this moment, you feel
two things really strongly.

00:08:01.460 --> 00:08:04.400
You feel a literal physical warmness,

00:08:04.400 --> 00:08:07.690
a wonder, a comfort, a
happiness in belonging.

00:08:07.690 --> 00:08:10.460
But the other feeling
you get is a yearning

00:08:10.460 --> 00:08:13.020
to be back in that moment

00:08:13.020 --> 00:08:16.060
and an acknowledgement that
it will never happen again.

00:08:16.060 --> 00:08:18.780
These two feelings are the ingredients

00:08:18.780 --> 00:08:21.843
for the potent experience of nostalgia.

00:08:30.910 --> 00:08:33.330
So when neuroscientists look at the brain

00:08:33.330 --> 00:08:35.870
of people who are experiencing nostalgia,

00:08:35.870 --> 00:08:38.290
they see something pretty interesting.

00:08:38.290 --> 00:08:41.890
They see these two spots that fire at once

00:08:41.890 --> 00:08:44.600
and sort of start talking to each other,

00:08:44.600 --> 00:08:47.410
a very unique process in the brain.

00:08:47.410 --> 00:08:50.960
This one is the mind recalling the memory.

00:08:50.960 --> 00:08:53.490
It's technically called
chronological remoteness,

00:08:53.490 --> 00:08:54.890
let's just call it memory.

00:08:54.890 --> 00:08:58.010
And one is your emotional network,

00:08:58.010 --> 00:09:01.230
the stuff that makes
you feel happy or sad.

00:09:01.230 --> 00:09:03.260
And during nostalgia specifically,

00:09:03.260 --> 00:09:05.750
it's firing in the reward centers.

00:09:05.750 --> 00:09:09.770
The good, the positive,
yes, brain likes this.

00:09:09.770 --> 00:09:14.250
These two fire together and
connect with each other.

00:09:14.250 --> 00:09:16.830
It's a strange neural dance

00:09:16.830 --> 00:09:18.920
between these two unlikely partners

00:09:18.920 --> 00:09:21.400
of memory and emotional reward.

00:09:21.400 --> 00:09:24.050
This feeling and reaction is more potent

00:09:24.050 --> 00:09:27.640
if one, the memory has
to do with belonging

00:09:27.640 --> 00:09:31.060
or personal attachment,
like friends and family,

00:09:31.060 --> 00:09:34.140
or if the feeling was a
compelling impactful moment

00:09:34.140 --> 00:09:37.130
in your life that had a
lot of personal meaning,

00:09:37.130 --> 00:09:41.400
maybe a big career thing or
a big life-changing moment.

00:09:41.400 --> 00:09:43.374
And number two, the
feeling is more intense

00:09:43.374 --> 00:09:47.030
the longer it's been since
you've thought about it.

00:09:47.030 --> 00:09:51.620
So like that feeling with
me on the beach in Tijuana,

00:09:51.620 --> 00:09:55.400
I literally had not thought
about that in eight years.

00:09:55.400 --> 00:09:57.110
And so looking at those photos,

00:09:57.110 --> 00:10:01.150
it was a compelling,
personal, meaningful moment.

00:10:01.150 --> 00:10:02.963
My brain was going wild.

00:10:04.160 --> 00:10:06.727
The studies on this don't
just look at the FMRIs

00:10:06.727 --> 00:10:08.950
and the brain and all of that stuff,

00:10:08.950 --> 00:10:10.490
which is really interesting,

00:10:10.490 --> 00:10:13.480
but it also looks at what actually happens

00:10:13.480 --> 00:10:15.800
in the current day life of the person

00:10:15.800 --> 00:10:17.500
who is experiencing nostalgia.

00:10:17.500 --> 00:10:18.900
What some of these studies are finding

00:10:18.900 --> 00:10:21.150
is that nostalgia can actually

00:10:21.150 --> 00:10:24.330
increase somebody's
personal view of themselves,

00:10:24.330 --> 00:10:26.830
their self-esteem, and it can help combat

00:10:26.830 --> 00:10:29.120
the effects of loneliness.

00:10:29.120 --> 00:10:31.200
As I cultivated nostalgia,

00:10:31.200 --> 00:10:34.300
I really started to understand why that is

00:10:34.300 --> 00:10:35.893
and how that actually works.

00:10:38.260 --> 00:10:41.760
I am listening to Weezer,
to an album that came out

00:10:41.760 --> 00:10:43.510
when I was a senior in high school,

00:10:45.390 --> 00:10:50.390
and it is just flooding my
world with this feeling.

00:10:52.170 --> 00:10:54.410
One of the things that
keeps coming back to me

00:10:54.410 --> 00:10:57.610
is the people, the people I was with,

00:10:57.610 --> 00:11:01.310
like the Friday nights I
would have with my friends.

00:11:01.310 --> 00:11:03.860
The place is there in my memory,

00:11:03.860 --> 00:11:05.993
but the people I was with,

00:11:06.840 --> 00:11:09.950
I feel their presence in some strange way

00:11:09.950 --> 00:11:13.210
as I cultivate these
memories, it's really weird.

00:11:13.210 --> 00:11:17.780
I see their faces, I feel
like I'm with them again.

00:11:17.780 --> 00:11:22.780
It's good and warm, but it's also hurtful.

00:11:22.930 --> 00:11:24.650
I found myself wanting
to turn this music off

00:11:24.650 --> 00:11:25.483
because I was like,

00:11:25.483 --> 00:11:27.140
"Oh, I don't want to go there right now."

00:11:27.140 --> 00:11:30.410
It's very, it's very
contradictory, these feelings.

00:11:30.410 --> 00:11:32.570
Anyway, back to it.

00:11:32.570 --> 00:11:34.670
This is in keeping with my broader theory

00:11:34.670 --> 00:11:37.390
about the stories we tell ourselves

00:11:37.390 --> 00:11:40.430
and how the information in our little ring

00:11:40.430 --> 00:11:42.820
is usually information we use to construct

00:11:42.820 --> 00:11:46.850
our conscious story, and
that introducing old memories

00:11:46.850 --> 00:11:50.010
that are positive or meaningful
or tell a different story

00:11:50.010 --> 00:11:54.250
than your current one can really
help you think differently

00:11:54.250 --> 00:11:57.610
about who you are and what your life is.

00:11:57.610 --> 00:12:02.610
Your conscious story becomes
richer and more flush

00:12:02.880 --> 00:12:06.390
with good experiences
and positive information

00:12:06.390 --> 00:12:08.650
about you and your life.

00:12:08.650 --> 00:12:12.180
So while I was doing this,
Nathaniel was also doing it

00:12:12.180 --> 00:12:15.133
on his side, but in a very different way.

00:12:16.540 --> 00:12:17.650
- We're off to a bad start here.

00:12:17.650 --> 00:12:18.483
- [Man] Where are we?

00:12:18.483 --> 00:12:19.780
- I have no idea.

00:12:19.780 --> 00:12:20.850
We're here actually.

00:12:20.850 --> 00:12:24.920
The last time I remember ever
even holding a map like this

00:12:24.920 --> 00:12:28.110
was with my mom 15 years ago.

00:12:28.110 --> 00:12:30.823
- Okay, but nostalgia has a dark side too.

00:12:33.240 --> 00:12:37.070
One of the big takeaways
from the studies on nostalgia

00:12:37.070 --> 00:12:39.930
find that people actually
lean on nostalgia

00:12:39.930 --> 00:12:41.350
during hard times.

00:12:41.350 --> 00:12:43.790
They'll use it as sort
of a coping mechanism

00:12:43.790 --> 00:12:46.870
and this sometimes can
be exploited by marketers

00:12:46.870 --> 00:12:50.030
or in a lot of cases, politicians.

00:12:50.030 --> 00:12:52.230
- Remember in the old days,
when a crime was a crime?

00:12:52.230 --> 00:12:55.490
- We will make America great again!

00:12:55.490 --> 00:12:57.520
- This happens in so
many different contexts,

00:12:57.520 --> 00:13:01.240
to the far right nationalistic
movements in Japan

00:13:01.240 --> 00:13:04.130
that are hearkening back
to the Japanese empire

00:13:04.130 --> 00:13:05.400
and how much better it was.

00:13:05.400 --> 00:13:09.280
You can also look at ISIS and
their focus on hearkening back

00:13:09.280 --> 00:13:11.750
to the glory days of the old caliphate.

00:13:11.750 --> 00:13:14.290
It happened here in this country in 2016

00:13:14.290 --> 00:13:16.920
with make America great again,

00:13:16.920 --> 00:13:21.870
hearkening back to some
ill-defined, fake, censored past

00:13:21.870 --> 00:13:24.670
that never really existed
but that helps people

00:13:24.670 --> 00:13:27.700
who are maybe struggling
with changing demographics

00:13:27.700 --> 00:13:30.690
in the country or losing power

00:13:30.690 --> 00:13:34.010
to feel like they have
something to latch onto.

00:13:34.010 --> 00:13:38.010
These political campaigns
exploit that same brain process

00:13:38.010 --> 00:13:41.250
that I experienced when
I look at old photos,

00:13:41.250 --> 00:13:45.463
but instead they utilize
it to mobilize supporters.

00:13:50.820 --> 00:13:52.830
So I did this for a month.

00:13:52.830 --> 00:13:57.550
I cultivated nostalgia every
day, listened to old music

00:13:57.550 --> 00:14:01.280
and read journals and looked
at old photos and footage,

00:14:01.280 --> 00:14:04.863
and I can say that I
definitely learned a lot.

00:14:05.930 --> 00:14:09.470
So during these strange
times of loneliness

00:14:09.470 --> 00:14:12.090
and non-social interaction,

00:14:12.090 --> 00:14:15.290
remember that your ring
is full of information

00:14:15.290 --> 00:14:19.060
that is not super indicative
of who you really are.

00:14:19.060 --> 00:14:23.420
If you're feeling lonely or disconnected,

00:14:23.420 --> 00:14:27.143
I recommend you experiment
with taking the edge off

